CAT = "mtguardPowers"; SEG = "POW"
# Criminal Code (Cap. 9) arts. 86, 223, 224, 355W, 355X, 355AB, 355AC; Private Guards and Community
# Officers Act (Cap. 389) arts. 12(3), 12A, 12B, 25(d). Read on legislation.mt, November 2026.
Q = [
("Which article of Malta's Criminal Code lets a person who is not a police officer arrest without a warrant?",
 "Article 355W", ["Article 355X", "Article 355AD", "Article 12A of Cap. 389"],
 "Article 355W(1) of the Criminal Code gives the power to any person not being a police officer. Article 355X is the police officer's power, which is wider."),
("Under article 355W, when may a guard arrest someone for theft?",
 "In the act or just after it", ["Any time within a week", "Only with a manager's consent", "Only if the value is high"],
 "Article 355W(1) covers anyone who is in the act of committing, or has just committed, a listed crime, including theft. It does not cover old offences or mere suspicion of a future one."),
("Which crime is NOT on the list in article 355W for an arrest by a private person?",
 "Selling goods unlicensed", ["Theft from a shop", "Wilful damage to property", "Wilful bodily harm"],
 "Article 355W lists sexual offences, wilful homicide or bodily harm, theft, and wilful unlawful entry or damage to property. Trading offences are left to the police."),
("A man has climbed a locked fence and forced a storeroom door a moment ago. May a guard arrest him under article 355W?",
 "Yes, unlawful entry is listed", ["No, only the police may", "No, as nothing was taken", "Only once he has left the site"],
 "Article 355W(1) includes any crime of wilful unlawful entry or damage to property, committed in front of the guard or just committed."),
("A guard only suspects a man is about to steal from a parked car. May he arrest him under article 355W?",
 "No, that power is for police", ["Yes, prevention is enough", "Yes, if the car is a client's", "Yes, if two guards agree"],
 "Article 355X lets a police officer arrest someone he reasonably suspects is about to commit a crime. Article 355W gives a private person power only over someone in the act or who has just committed a listed crime. The guard should watch and call the police."),
("After arresting a thief, what must a guard do without delay under article 355W(2)?",
 "Inform the Police", ["Take a photo for the client", "Search his home", "Ask the shop for a statement"],
 "Article 355W(2) says the person making the arrest shall without delay inform the Police of the arrest."),
("How long may a guard hold a person arrested under article 355W?",
 "Until police take over", ["Up to twenty-four hours", "Until the shop closes", "Until the goods are paid for"],
 "Article 355W(2) says the power may be exercised only until it is strictly necessary for the Police to take over the person arrested."),
("Under article 355AC, what must a person under arrest be told, even if it is obvious?",
 "That he is under arrest", ["The guard's home address", "The value of the goods", "The client's name"],
 "Article 355AC(1) says an arrest is not lawful unless the person is informed that he is under arrest, even though the arrest may be obvious."),
("Under article 355AC, how must the reasons for an arrest be given?",
 "In a language he understands", ["In Maltese, the national language", "In writing, signed by the guard", "In English, as the court language"],
 "Article 355AC(2) requires the reasons to be given in a language the person understands. Where a private person makes the arrest, the reasons may be given once the Police take over."),
("Which kind of restraint does article 355AB allow when arresting someone?",
 "Only what is indispensable", ["Handcuffs in every arrest", "Any restraint the client wants", "None at all, ever"],
 "Article 355AB says the person making an arrest shall not use any harshness, bond or other means of restraint unless indispensably required to secure the person or made necessary by his insubordination."),
("A guard locks a teenager in a back room for an hour to frighten him, with no lawful ground. Which offence may this be?",
 "Unlawful arrest, article 86", ["Trespass by the guard", "A breach of the peace", "No offence, as he is a guard"],
 "Article 86 of the Criminal Code punishes whoever, without a lawful order and outside the cases where the law lets private individuals apprehend offenders, arrests, detains or confines a person against his will."),
("Under article 223 of the Criminal Code, when is bodily harm not an offence?",
 "In lawful defence of a person", ["When the client orders it", "When the person is very rude", "When stolen goods are recovered"],
 "Article 223 says no offence is committed when bodily harm is imposed by actual necessity in lawful self-defence or in the lawful defence of another person."),
("A guard punches a shoplifter after the man has already surrendered. Is this lawful defence?",
 "No, there was no necessity", ["Yes, he is a thief", "Yes, if the guard was angry", "Yes, if no CCTV saw it"],
 "Lawful defence under article 223 rests on actual necessity. Once the threat is over, force becomes punishment, which no guard may give."),
("At a place of entertainment, whom may a licensed guard refuse entry under article 12A?",
 "Someone violent through drink", ["Anyone without a ticket stub", "Anyone the guard dislikes", "Anyone who is not from the locality"],
 "Article 12A(1) of Cap. 389 lets a guard at a place of entertainment refuse entry to a person known for disturbance, one who refuses to produce identification, one manifestly intent on trouble, or one who appears violent because of intoxication."),
("A patron at a club refuses to show an identification document when asked. What does article 12A(1) allow?",
 "Entry may be refused", ["He may be searched", "He may be arrested", "His phone may be kept"],
 "Article 12A(1) of Cap. 389 says entry may be denied to any person who refuses to produce an identification document on request."),
("A patron becomes violent inside a bar. What must the guard at the place of entertainment do first under article 12A(2)?",
 "Notify the Police immediately", ["Eject him by the back door", "Call the owner's lawyer first", "Photograph him for the file"],
 "Article 12A(2) says the guard shall take measures to notify the Police immediately and may detain the person until a Police officer arrives."),
("A patron keeps harassing a woman in a club. Under article 12A(3), the guard may:",
 "Escort him out or detain him", ["Fine him on the spot", "Keep his identity card overnight", "Ban him from every club"],
 "Article 12A(3) of Cap. 389 says the guard shall notify the Police immediately and may escort the person outside the premises or detain him until a Police officer arrives."),
("When may a guard at a place of entertainment use force under article 12A(4)?",
 "If he will not comply otherwise", ["Whenever the venue owner asks him to", "Whenever a patron swears", "Whenever the venue is full"],
 "Article 12A(4) says the guard may exercise minimum force only where the person fails to comply without physical restraint."),
("What kind of force does Cap. 389 allow a guard at a place of entertainment?",
 "Minimum force", ["Reasonable punishment", "Any force short of injury", "Force the client approves"],
 "Articles 12A(4) and 12B(5) both say minimum force, and only where the person fails to comply without physical restraint. Article 25(d) makes excessive force an offence."),
("Which guard may search people entering a guarded site for weapons under Cap. 389?",
 "A specialised private guard", ["Any licensed private guard", "A community officer on patrol", "A trainee on his first week"],
 "Article 12B(2) gives a specialised private guard the power to search any person entering the premises for arms or objects that may be used for offence or defence."),
("A specialised guard finds a knife on a person at the entrance. What does article 12B(2) require?",
 "Notify police, keep it, detain", ["Hand it back at the exit", "Throw it into a bin outside", "Let him in as long as he stays calm"],
 "Article 12B(2) says the guard shall immediately notify the Police, withhold the object and detain the person until a Police officer arrives."),
("Near a cash-in-transit drop, a man shouts threats and shows a blade. What may a specialised guard do?",
 "Detain him until police come", ["Chase him off the street", "Search his car at his home", "Only note the car's number plate"],
 "Article 12B(4) lets a specialised guard guarding money in transit detain anyone in the area of operation who is threatening or displays a weapon, until a Police officer arrives, and notify the Police immediately."),
("Which offence does article 25(d) of Cap. 389 create for guards with special powers?",
 "Excessive use of force", ["Arriving late for a shift", "Losing a radio", "Refusing overtime"],
 "Article 25(d) makes it an offence for a private guard at a place of entertainment or a specialised private guard to use excessive force or otherwise abuse the powers the Act gives."),
("An ordinary private guard at an office building wants to check a visitor's bag. On what basis can he?",
 "The visitor's consent", ["His licence card alone", "Article 355W of the Code", "Any suspicion he has"],
 "Article 12(3) of Cap. 389 says a licence adds no rights over third parties. Without a power such as article 12B, a bag check rests on consent, often a condition of entry the visitor may refuse by not entering."),
("A sign at a Valletta museum says bags may be checked at entry. A tourist agrees and opens his bag. Who should handle the items?",
 "The tourist himself", ["The guard, with bare hands", "Another tourist", "The ticket seller"],
 "Asking the owner to move items himself avoids accusations of damage or theft and protects the guard from sharp objects."),
("A person walks onto a private car park without permission but commits no crime. What should a guard do?",
 "Ask him to leave politely", ["Arrest him for trespass", "Lock the car park gate behind him", "Take his car keys away"],
 "Simply being on private land is not on the article 355W list. The guard asks the person to leave, records it and calls the police if a crime follows."),
("Who may a private guard arrest using police powers under article 355X?",
 "Nobody, 355X is for police", ["Anyone suspected of a crime", "Anyone after 10 p.m.", "Anyone on the client's site"],
 "Article 355X gives powers to any police officer. A private guard has only the private person's power in article 355W."),
("A guard sees a man punch a stranger in a hotel lobby and run. The guard catches him a minute later. Is the arrest lawful?",
 "Yes, he just committed it", ["No, guards cannot arrest", "No, only the victim may", "Only inside the guard's post"],
 "Wilful bodily harm is on the article 355W list and the man has just committed it. The guard must inform the Police without delay and hold him only until they take over."),
("While holding an arrested person for the police, what must the guard look after?",
 "His safety and wellbeing", ["Getting a full confession", "Taking his fingerprints", "Collecting compensation"],
 "A person held is in the guard's care until the police take over. The guard watches for injury or illness and calls 112 for medical help if needed; questioning is for the police."),
("Under article 224, which act is listed as a case of lawful defence?",
 "Defence against violent theft", ["Chasing a fleeing graffiti artist", "Recovering a stolen bicycle later", "Punishing a rude customer"],
 "Article 224(b) of the Criminal Code includes harm caused in defence against a person committing theft or plunder with violence, or attempting it. Force after the event is not defence."),
("A client asks a guard to carry out a task a police officer would do, such as setting up a roadblock on a public road. How should the guard reply?",
 "That a licence gives no such power", ["That he will do it in uniform", "That he needs a written order", "That it is fine at night"],
 "Article 12(3) of Cap. 389 says a licence gives no right or privilege in relation to third parties that the holder did not have before. Controlling a public road belongs to the police."),
("A suspect asks why he is being held while the guard waits for the police. What does article 355AC allow a private person?",
 "To give them once police take over", ["To refuse ever to give them", "To give them the next morning", "To let the client explain them later on"],
 "Article 355AC(2) lets the information be delayed until the person arrested is taken over by the Police when the arrest is made by a private person, though he must still be told he is under arrest."),
]
